\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Finite Green identities survive curvature}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For the \vocabk{transported boundary form}{fdip-00T1},
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal B_M(v,w)
&=\langle v,(\extD_UP_M-P_M\extD_U)w\rangle\\
&=-\mathcal B_{M^c}(v,w).
\end{aligned}}
It depends only on the restrictions of #{v,w} to the declared
boundary support. If a \vocabk{kernel complement}{fdip-00T1}
is given, then #{E_U,H_U} are self-adjoint and
##{\extD_UE_U=E_U\extD_U=\bbI-H_U,\qquad
E_UH_U=H_UE_U=0.}
For every cochain #{v} and test cochain #{\gamma},
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle v,\gamma\rangle_M
={}&\langle\extD_Uv,E_U\gamma\rangle_M
+\langle v,H_U\gamma\rangle_M\\
&-\mathcal B_M(v,E_U\gamma).
\end{aligned}}
On all cells this yields #{v=E_U\extD_Uv+H_Uv}. A kernel
complement always exists over an ordered field with a positive
definite symmetric pairing, and over #{\mathbb C} with a
positive definite Hermitian pairing. None of these conclusions
requires flatness.}

\proof{
\p{The coefficient projection #{P_M} is self-adjoint. Moving
#{\extD_U} through the full pairing gives the commutator
formula. Its diagonal blocks relative to #{M\sqcup M^c}
vanish; only the two cross blocks remain. Removing cells that
occur in neither nonzero cross block changes no summand.
Replacing #{P_M} by #{\bbI-P_M} negates the commutator and
proves the complementary identity. This argument uses only
finite sums and perfect adjointness.}

\p{For a declared complement, invariance and invertibility give
#{W=\im\extD_U}. Indeed #{\extD_U} kills its
kernel and maps #{W} onto #{W}. If #{h\in\ker\extD_U} and
#{w=\extD_Uz\in W}, self-adjointness gives
##{\langle h,w\rangle=\langle\extD_Uh,z\rangle=0,\qquad
\langle w,h\rangle=\langle z,\extD_Uh\rangle=0.}
The direct sum is therefore orthogonal. Its restricted
pairings are perfect: the map to the dual is block diagonal
for this sum and is an isomorphism on the whole module, so
both diagonal maps are isomorphisms. Projection onto the
kernel is self-adjoint. On #{W}, for #{u,w\in W},
##{\langle E_Uu,w\rangle
=\langle E_Uu,\extD_UE_Uw\rangle
=\langle u,E_Uw\rangle.}
Both sides vanish if one argument lies in the kernel. Hence
#{E_U} is self-adjoint on all cochains. Evaluating on each
direct summand gives both inverse identities and the zero
products with #{H_U}.}

\p{Substitute #{w=E_U\gamma} into the definition of
#{\mathcal B_M} and use
#{\extD_UE_U\gamma=\gamma-H_U\gamma}; rearrangement gives
the displayed formula. For all cells the boundary form is
zero. Move the self-adjoint maps to the first argument and
use perfectness to obtain #{v=E_U\extD_Uv+H_Uv}.}

\p{Finally, over a positive field let #{K=\ker\extD_U}.
Restriction of the pairing identifies each finite-dimensional
subspace with its dual, so #{C^*=K\oplus K^\perp}, by the
rank argument in \ref{fdip-00P6}.
Self-adjointness makes #{K^\perp} invariant. The restriction
of #{\extD_U} to it has zero kernel and hence is bijective.
Taking #{W=K^\perp} supplies the required complement.}
}

\p{Labeled face occurrences bound the boundary support, but
ordinary cancelled cellular boundary coefficients need not
detect it. For a period-one loop the two ordinary endpoints
cancel, whereas #{\rho(U)^{-1}-\bbI} can be nonzero. This is
why support above is defined by the actual transported blocks.
Over a general ring an arbitrary complement, without the
stated invariance and invertibility, does not supply this inverse.}
